# BSGGCam ships with genuinely compressed JNI libraries (confirmed via the
# upstream APK itself, not a packaging mistake here), which is fine for a
# regular sideloaded/updated app -- PackageManagerService extracts
# compressed native libs to /data/app/.../lib at install time. It does
# NOT do that for a system app that was never "updated"
# (PackageAbiHelperImpl.shouldExtractLibs(): "We shouldn't attempt to
# extract libs from system app when it was not updated"), which is
# exactly what a PRODUCT_PACKAGES-preloaded system app is on first boot.
# Without extraction, every native lib in the apk is unloadable, crashing
# the app at first launch (observed: "dlopen failed: library
# "libpairipcore.so" not found", just the first .so it happens to load).
#
# The APK's own code (Pairip app-protection) verifies its own signing
# certificate at runtime, so it cannot be re-signed or have its zip
# entries' compression touched -- the fix has to work on the byte-for-
# byte original file. `pm install -r` over the already-installed app's
# own apk path re-installs it through the normal install code path
# (isUpdatedSystemApp becomes true for that install), which does extract
# the native libs, without changing a single byte of the apk itself.
# Harmless to repeat every boot (quick no-op once libs are extracted).
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    exec_background - system system -- /system/bin/pm install -r /system/app/BSGGCam/BSGGCam.apk
